---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="False-Green CI Checks in AI Agent Workflows · San Lee"
  description="Six CI checks reported success, and the required AI-agent work never ran. This page carries the contract-level fixes that made the gates trustworthy."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/false-green.html"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/assets/og-false-green.png"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Two-ways-to-fail schematic (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: the oxide
       FAIL vs ink PASS encoding is unique to this claim, and the diagram does
       not borrow series colours the way the loop-replay split map does. */
    .two-fail {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 8px 0 10px;
    }
    /* Neutral step boxes. The fill reads --bg so the box sits in the page and
       not on a card. Inside a .fig wrapper the figure ground scopes --bg, so
       the same fill tracks the lifted night ground (ADR-014). The stroke reads
       --stroke-figure, which is the figure layer's strong rank, so the night
       retune reaches this plate and nothing outside it (A2, PL-10). */
    .two-fail .tf-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    /* Shared empty middle: hollow + dashed. Same geometry on both lanes so the
       only difference that reads is the outcome badge and the final edge. */
    .two-fail .tf-hollow {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-fail {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-pass {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-step {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 12px;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-hollow-label {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11.5px;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-fail-text {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-pass-text {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Loud break into FAIL: oxide stroke, short segments with a real gap. */
    .two-fail .tf-edge-break {
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      fill: none;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-edge-pass {
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .two-fail .tf-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 10px;
    }
    .two-fail marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .two-fail marker path.tf-head-fail { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
    .two-fail marker path.tf-head-pass { fill: var(--text); }

    /* Six-pass plate: the count, not the mechanism (two-fail is that).
       Six identical PASS marks. Oxide + word: they passed. ADR-010 N/A. */
    .six-pass {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .six-pass .sx-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .six-pass .sx-ok {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .six-pass .sx-ok-text {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    /* The caption box for the two BARE plates on this page.

       The two-slot caption grammar moved to public/assets/style.css with the
       MONOGRAPH identity (ADR-014, OC-F closed), so the page-scoped copy of it
       is deleted. The shared grammar gives .fig-what its note voice through a
       descendant selector, and it gives the caption BOX its measure through
       `.fig figcaption`. A bare plate wraps in a <div class="fig fig--plate">
       and therefore holds no <figcaption>, so this rule declares that box for
       the two plates that need it.

       A bare plate never becomes a <figure>. Gate rule R-C makes a declared
       figure owe a two-slot caption, and a limit sentence written to answer a
       gate is a claim that nobody measured. */
    .fig--plate > .fig-what {
      max-width: var(--content);
      margin: 14px auto 0;
    }

    /* fg-s1. The three attempts, re-laid as a strip. Each row keeps the words
       the paragraph already used: the break, then what CI reported. The strip
       re-lays the text. It does not rewrite it.

       The cost, the refused posts, and the recorded denial count stay in the
       prose below the strip. A dollar figure never enters a figure on this
       site (redesign brief 1.12, OC-D), and the one on this page keeps the
       placement its owner already chose.

       The row stacks below 34rem and becomes two columns above it, so a 320px
       reader never reads a squeezed cell. min-width: 0 on both cells, because
       a grid item defaults to min-content and a long token then widens the
       page. The cells carry no link, so hit-target.cjs measures nothing new. */
    .fg-s1-rows {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 8px 0 0;
      padding: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .fg-s1-rows li {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 2px 24px;
      padding: 10px 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .fg-s1-rows .fgs-break {
      min-width: 0;
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .fg-s1-rows .fgs-out {
      min-width: 0;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
    @media (min-width: 34rem) {
      .fg-s1-rows li {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 13rem;
        align-items: start;
      }
      .fg-s1-rows .fgs-out {
        justify-self: end;
        text-align: right;
      }
    }

    /* fg1-keys. One result message, two key reads (ADR-013). Page-scoped.

       max-width 260 keeps one viewBox unit at one CSS pixel down to 320px. The
       plate sits inside a .decision card, and that card adds no horizontal
       padding, so a 320px phone leaves it the same 272px the .wrap gutter
       leaves. That is the measurement the 1:1 rule needs.

       Oxide marks the miss, and this page already reads oxide that way: the
       six-pass marks and the two-fail FAIL badge both carry it. The miss also
       runs dashed behind a broken edge, so the state is a word plus a shape
       and never a colour alone. No glyph replaces those words, because Geist
       ships none for the symbols that would.

       The marker ids carry the page code and a digit (fg1-arrow), because a
       marker id is global to the document. A duplicate id repaints the wrong
       arrow head, and no gate finds that. */
    .fg1-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 14px 0 10px;
    }
    .fg1-plate .fg1-title {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    /* The object box and the two read boxes carry the figure layer's strong
       rank (A2, PL-10), so the dark retune reaches them. The fill reads --bg,
       which the figure ground scopes, so each box tracks the lifted night
       ground instead of punching a darker hole in it. */
    .fg1-plate .fg1-obj {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .fg1-plate .fg1-obj-label {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11.5px;
    }
    .fg1-plate .fg1-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Broken stub into the miss: the gap reads before the colour does. */
    .fg1-plate .fg1-edge-miss {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .fg1-plate .fg1-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .fg1-plate .fg1-box-miss {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    .fg1-plate .fg1-key {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 10px;
    }
    .fg1-plate .fg1-hit {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .fg1-plate .fg1-miss {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .fg1-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .fg1-plate marker path.fg1-head-miss { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />

  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="../index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>False Green</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">I built six checks and wired them into CI. Later I found that each one reported success for work that never ran. Each one exited green.</p>

    <!-- Plate 01. A bare plate takes a <div class="fig fig--plate"> wrapper and
         never a <figure> (ADR-014). Gate rule R-C makes a declared <figure> owe
         a two-slot caption, and this plate's caption states what it shows and
         nothing more. data-place="spine" is the per-kind default for a plate:
         it pins the plate to the content track. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate" data-place="spine">
      <span class="fig-label">Plate 01</span>
      <svg class="six-pass" viewBox="0 0 260 100" role="img"
           aria-label="Six checks. Each one reported PASS for work that never ran.">
        <title>Six PASS marks. The shared fact is that they passed.</title>
        <text class="sx-lane" x="4" y="12">SIX CHECKS</text>
        <rect class="sx-ok" x="4" y="22" width="80" height="28" rx="2" />
        <text class="sx-ok-text" x="44" y="40" text-anchor="middle">PASS</text>
        <rect class="sx-ok" x="90" y="22" width="80" height="28" rx="2" />
        <text class="sx-ok-text" x="130" y="40" text-anchor="middle">PASS</text>
        <rect class="sx-ok" x="176" y="22" width="80" height="28" rx="2" />
        <text class="sx-ok-text" x="216" y="40" text-anchor="middle">PASS</text>
        <rect class="sx-ok" x="4" y="58" width="80" height="28" rx="2" />
        <text class="sx-ok-text" x="44" y="76" text-anchor="middle">PASS</text>
        <rect class="sx-ok" x="90" y="58" width="80" height="28" rx="2" />
        <text class="sx-ok-text" x="130" y="76" text-anchor="middle">PASS</text>
        <rect class="sx-ok" x="176" y="58" width="80" height="28" rx="2" />
        <text class="sx-ok-text" x="216" y="76" text-anchor="middle">PASS</text>
      </svg>
      <p class="fig-what">
        Six greens. Each check reported PASS for work that never ran.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- The long-page nav device (ADR-014). The re-based ToC gate carries this
         page on the SCREEN arm, not the figure arm. The gate opens at 8 phone
         screens or 6 figures. The page holds four figures, so the figure arm
         stays shut. It ran 9.08 phone screens at 393px before this device
         landed, so the screen arm decides. The page was already over the gate
         on main; this lane is the first to apply it here.

         The device is typographic and it runs no JavaScript. The Contents rows
         index the six sections. The nav carries id="toc" because the Contents
         returns below target it (PL-8: a #toc link ships only on a page that
         holds the id, and link-check.cjs does not read same-page fragments).

         This page ships NO .toc-figs comma-run, and the reason is measured.
         The graft sets the run `white-space: nowrap` beside the section link,
         which suits a short section title. This page's titles run to 51
         characters and they wrap at 320px, so the run lands beside the FIRST
         line of a wrapping title and reads as part of that title. It also
         ends at 296px in a track that ends at 296px, which is a fit with no
         slack. The figure numbers already ride each figure in flow, which is
         where the reader meets them. Plate 01 sits above this nav and inside
         no section, so no row would index it in any case. -->
    <nav class="toc" id="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-heading">
      <p class="toc-heading" id="toc-heading">On this page</p>
      <ol>
      <li><a href="#two-ways-to-fail">Two ways to fail</a></li>
      <li><a href="#measured-the-wrong-thing">The check that measured the wrong thing</a></li>
      <li><a href="#could-not-see">The check that could not see what it was looking for</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-each-one-measured">What each one was actually measuring</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-check-watching-the-check">The check watching the check</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-question">The question</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="two-ways-to-fail">Two ways to fail</h2>

    <p>
      The first agentic review lane I put into CI broke three times before it
      worked.
    </p>

    <!-- fg-s1, the attempts strip. Three breaks of one lane, each beside what
         CI reported. The rows re-lay the three paragraphs that stood here. The
         STE conversion of the same words is a separate pass in the same commit,
         and the pull request keeps the two ledger blocks apart.

         Everything the row grammar cannot hold stays in the paragraph below:
         the cost, the refused posts, the recorded denial count, and the ruling
         that a tool denial is not a job failure. The dollar figure never enters
         the figure (redesign brief 1.12, OC-D).

         Strip 02 in the page's figure order. data-place="spine" is the
         per-kind default for a strip: it pins the strip to the content track,
         beside the prose that carries what the rows cannot hold. -->
    <figure class="fg-s1 fig" data-fig="strip" data-place="spine">
      <span class="fig-label">Strip 02</span>
      <ul class="fg-s1-rows">
        <li>
          <span class="fgs-break">A missing OIDC permission</span>
          <span class="fgs-out">Red in minutes.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="fgs-break">A missing tool grant</span>
          <span class="fgs-out">Green in thirty-six seconds.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="fgs-break">A pull request that edited the lane&rsquo;s own workflow file</span>
          <span class="fgs-out">Green. The job never ran at all.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">One agentic review lane broke three times
        before it worked. The first break failed in minutes. The second break
        and the third break both reported green.</span>
        <span class="fig-limit">The strip holds the outcome and nothing else.
        The second break&rsquo;s cost, its refused posts, and the recorded
        denial count stay in the prose below.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      That second run spent $0.14 across seven turns. The agent read the diff,
      formed a review, and attempted to post it six times. Every attempt was
      refused. The agent exited zero. The run recorded
      <code>permission_denials_count: 6</code>. A tool denial is not a job
      failure, so the job did not fail.
    </p>

    <!-- Argument: same empty middle, opposite report. The state is a word plus
         a geometry first (FAIL/PASS, a broken or an intact edge). Oxide and ink
         come second. This is not a colour-only reading, and the figure invents
         no CI green.

         Plate 03 in the page's figure order. It is a bare plate, so it takes a
         <div class="fig fig--plate"> wrapper and never a <figure> (ADR-014).
         The svg keeps class="two-fail": figure-contract-baseline.json names
         this plate by that class, and a renamed class is an exemption that
         nobody granted. Its two text values stay byte-exact for the same
         reason. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate" data-place="spine">
      <span class="fig-label">Plate 03</span>
      <svg class="two-fail" viewBox="0 0 260 188" role="img"
           aria-label="Two parallel CI outcomes when no work ran. Top: a deterministic tool exits non-zero and reports FAIL. Bottom: an agentic or hand-written gate exits zero and reports PASS. The mark is the same whether the work was denied, crashed, or never attempted.">
        <defs>
          <marker id="tf-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
          <marker id="tf-arrow-fail" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path class="tf-head-fail" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
          <marker id="tf-arrow-pass" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path class="tf-head-pass" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <!-- Top lane: deterministic tool. A loud FAIL when nothing ran. -->
        <text class="tf-lane" x="6" y="14">DETERMINISTIC TOOL</text>
        <rect class="tf-box" x="6" y="24" width="64" height="36" rx="2" />
        <text class="tf-step" x="38" y="46" text-anchor="middle">run tool</text>
        <path class="tf-edge" d="M 70 42 L 84 42" marker-end="url(#tf-arrow)" />
        <rect class="tf-hollow" x="88" y="24" width="74" height="36" rx="2" />
        <text class="tf-hollow-label" x="125" y="46" text-anchor="middle">nothing ran</text>
        <!-- Broken final edge: the gap is the loud break into FAIL. -->
        <path class="tf-edge-break" d="M 162 42 L 170 42" />
        <path class="tf-edge-break" d="M 180 42 L 188 42" marker-end="url(#tf-arrow-fail)" />
        <!-- ASCII exit code + word badge only: ≠ / ✕ / ✓ are not in Geist, and
             FAIL/PASS + broken edge already dual-encode outcome (font-coverage). -->
        <text class="tf-micro" x="175" y="72" text-anchor="middle">exit != 0</text>
        <rect class="tf-fail" x="192" y="24" width="62" height="36" rx="2" />
        <text class="tf-fail-text" x="223" y="46" text-anchor="middle">FAIL</text>

        <!-- Bottom lane: agentic or hand gate. A quiet PASS on the same empty middle. -->
        <text class="tf-lane" x="6" y="104">AGENTIC / HAND GATE</text>
        <rect class="tf-box" x="6" y="114" width="64" height="36" rx="2" />
        <text class="tf-step" x="38" y="136" text-anchor="middle">run check</text>
        <path class="tf-edge" d="M 70 132 L 84 132" marker-end="url(#tf-arrow)" />
        <rect class="tf-hollow" x="88" y="114" width="74" height="36" rx="2" />
        <text class="tf-hollow-label" x="125" y="136" text-anchor="middle">nothing ran</text>
        <!-- Intact final edge: quiet false success. -->
        <path class="tf-edge tf-edge-pass" d="M 162 132 L 188 132" marker-end="url(#tf-arrow-pass)" />
        <text class="tf-micro" x="175" y="162" text-anchor="middle">exit 0</text>
        <rect class="tf-pass" x="192" y="114" width="62" height="36" rx="2" />
        <text class="tf-pass-text" x="223" y="136" text-anchor="middle">PASS</text>
      </svg>
      <p class="fig-what">
        Same empty middle, opposite report. A deterministic tool fails when it
        does nothing. An agentic or hand-written gate can still report PASS,
        whether the work happened, was denied, crashed, or was never attempted.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      I wrote that down as a lesson about agents. The same shape already sat in
      gates that I wrote by hand. It took another four months to find them.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="measured-the-wrong-thing">The check that measured the wrong thing</h2>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The gate that tested an invisible page</p>
      <h3>Mobile overflow, measured on unstyled pages</h3>
      <p>
        The mobile QA gate renders every page at four widths and fails on
        horizontal overflow. It opened the built files directly, over
        <code>file:</code> URLs. The layout emits root-absolute asset paths, and
        those paths do not resolve over <code>file:</code>. So every page it
        measured rendered unstyled.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The result:</strong> an unstyled page does not overflow. The gate
        ran and produced numbers for sixteen pages at four widths. The numbers
        described a page no visitor would ever see. It now serves the build over
        HTTP.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The proof that proved something else</p>
      <h3>&ldquo;Fonts embedded: Geist&rdquo;</h3>
      <p>
        The r&eacute;sum&eacute; PDF script printed that line and I believed it
        for months. It read the value from <code>document.fonts</code>. That
        value proves a woff2 loaded into the <strong>browser</strong>. It says
        nothing about the bytes written to the PDF.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The result:</strong> it read as evidence. It cost me a round of
        investigation into a font defect that did not exist. It now asserts
        against the raw bytes of the file it just wrote.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Both produced output. A crash is obvious. These numbers looked like
      evidence.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="could-not-see">The check that could not see what it was looking for</h2>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Every deep link, unchecked</p>
      <h3>The link checker split the fragment off first</h3>
      <p>
        One line: split the URL on <code>#</code>, split on <code>?</code>, then
        ask whether the file exists. So a link to a real page with a dead anchor
        was a good link. The reader lands at the top of the page with no sign
        that anything went wrong.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The result:</strong> the checker did this from the day I wrote
        it. So it reported every deep link on the site as valid, and it never
        checked one anchor. It mattered the moment I moved sections between
        pages. That was the three pull requests immediately before I found it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>A face the gate cannot see</p>
      <h3>The font gate&rsquo;s regular expression</h3>
      <p>
        The coverage gate matches font declarations with
        <code>@font-face\s*&#123;([^&#125;]*)&#125;</code>. A comment between
        <code>@font-face</code> and its opening brace hides the block from
        <code>\s*</code>. A closing brace inside a comment truncates the body
        early.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The result:</strong> either way the face falls out of the scan
        through a silent <code>continue</code>. The expected face count then
        treats that gap as a pass.
        Against the fixture I wrote afterward, the old pattern sees one face out
        of three.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="what-each-one-measured">What each one was actually measuring</h2>

    <table class="metrics">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">The check</th>
          <th scope="col">Appeared to measure</th>
          <th scope="col">Actually measured</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Mobile overflow</th>
          <td>Every page at four widths</td>
          <td>Unstyled pages, which cannot overflow</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">R&eacute;sum&eacute; fonts</th>
          <td>Fonts embedded in the PDF</td>
          <td>Fonts loaded into the browser</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Link checker</th>
          <td>Every link resolves</td>
          <td>Every link&rsquo;s page exists</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Font coverage</th>
          <td>Every declared face</td>
          <td>The faces its pattern could see</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Review lane</th>
          <td>A code review happened</td>
          <td>The job exited zero</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Denial counter</th>
          <td>Tool denials in the run</td>
          <td>A key that does not exist</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="the-check-watching-the-check">The check watching the check</h2>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The instrument that read an absent field</p>
      <h3>A counter that was structurally always zero</h3>
      <p>
        I built the review workflow to warn me about exactly the failure that
        started this page. If a run hit tool denials, the classify step would
        report it. Then nobody could ever again read a green check with no
        comment as a clean review.
      </p>

      <!-- fg1-keys. One result message, two key reads. The hit returns an
           array and the miss returns nothing, so the code defaulted it to
           zero. Zero digits in the plate: the run figures stay in the prose
           below, where a reader can check them against the run summary. Both
           key names are exact, because a near-miss identifier is a wrong
           identifier and the whole defect is one character run of them.

           Plate 04 in the page's figure order. data-place="spine" states the
           per-kind width policy for a plate. It changes nothing here: this
           figure is nested inside a .decision card, so the wide-promotion list
           never reaches it and the card's own measure holds (A5). The
           attribute records the policy where the next reader looks for it. -->
      <figure class="fg1-fig fig" data-fig="plate" data-place="spine">
        <span class="fig-label">Plate 04</span>
        <svg class="fg1-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 118" role="img"
             aria-label="One result message and two key reads. The key permission_denials holds an array. The key permission_denials_count is not in the message, so the read returned zero.">
          <title>Two key reads against one result message. One returns an array, one returns zero.</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="fg1-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                    markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
              <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
            </marker>
            <marker id="fg1-arrow-miss" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                    markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
              <path class="fg1-head-miss" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
            </marker>
          </defs>

          <!-- The object both reads run against. -->
          <text class="fg1-title" x="6" y="11">ONE RESULT MESSAGE</text>
          <rect class="fg1-obj" x="6" y="16" width="248" height="22" rx="2" />
          <text class="fg1-obj-label" x="130" y="31" text-anchor="middle">the saved execution log</text>
          <path class="fg1-edge" d="M 16 38 L 16 96" />

          <!-- The read that hit: intact edge, solid box, a word for the value. -->
          <path class="fg1-edge" d="M 16 60 L 30 60" marker-end="url(#fg1-arrow)" />
          <rect class="fg1-box" x="34" y="48" width="222" height="24" rx="2" />
          <text class="fg1-key" x="42" y="64">permission_denials</text>
          <text class="fg1-hit" x="248" y="64" text-anchor="end">an array</text>

          <!-- The read that missed: broken edge, dashed box, and the word the
               code substituted. -->
          <path class="fg1-edge-miss" d="M 16 96 L 21 96" />
          <path class="fg1-edge-miss" d="M 25 96 L 30 96" marker-end="url(#fg1-arrow-miss)" />
          <rect class="fg1-box-miss" x="34" y="84" width="222" height="24" rx="2" />
          <text class="fg1-key" x="42" y="100">permission_denials_count</text>
          <text class="fg1-miss" x="248" y="100" text-anchor="end">zero</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          <span class="fig-what">The counter read one key and the message
          carried another. The array permission_denials was there. The key
          permission_denials_count was not, so the read returned undefined and
          the code defaulted it to zero.</span>
          <span class="fig-limit">The plate shows one defect, not the way two
          defects concealed each other. The run&rsquo;s logged denial count, its
          turn count, and its cost stay in the prose below.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The log is the raw message stream. Its result message carries the array
        <code>permission_denials</code>. A read of an absent key returns
        undefined, and the code defaulted that to zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        On one pull request the step logged <code>denials=0</code>. It reported
        <code>turns</code> as 8 and <code>cost</code> as 0.1447. Both figures
        matched the run summary to the digit. The same result object, and one
        field silently misread.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The result:</strong> that review agent held four tools, and none
        of them could open a file. The diff&rsquo;s meaning lived in the markup.
        The agent burned five denied reads and posted nothing. The instrument I
        built to report those denials read a field that was never there. The
        two defects concealed each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        The lane&rsquo;s earlier configuration was worse. For ten days it had no
        publish channel at all. It posted zero comments on zero pull requests.
        That looked like a quiet stretch with nothing to say.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

    <p>
      Each defect is one line. A passing check and a check that cannot fail look
      the same from the outside. I found each one. I asked a specific question
      of a specific gate.
    </p>

    <p class="flow-line">
      What is this green measuring <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span> and can
      it tell &ldquo;passed&rdquo; from &ldquo;never ran&rdquo;?
    </p>

    <p>
      The cheap version reconciles the gate&rsquo;s own reported count against a
      raw grep, once. Every gate on this site now asserts a non-zero count of
      the thing it walked. A gate pointed at an empty directory finds nothing
      and reports that as success.
    </p>

    <p class="tradeoff">
      <strong>One of them did not, and this page said so first.</strong> The
      metrics gate skipped its parity check on Markdown. So a Markdown marker
      that parsed to nothing looked exactly like a file that makes no claims. A
      wrong mark on a number was quieter than no mark at all. This page named
      that gap while it was still live.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is closed now. The Markdown branch counts its own markers. A suite
      asserts that the counter reports a gap for each shape the value pattern
      cannot read. A parity counter that returns zero and a correct parity
      counter produce identical output on a clean tree. So the fix was
      unfalsifiable until something made it fail on purpose. If I revert the
      one-line exemption, exactly three of those tests turn red.
    </p>

